{
  "id": "code_completion/templates/07_deduced_variables",
  "topic": "code_completion",
  "subtopic": "templates",
  "title": "Deduced variable members",
  "summary": "A variable declared `auto` from a dependent initializer completes the members of the class it deduces to",
  "prose": "`auto& row = rows[0]; row.` lists the members of `Vec<T>`. The declarator applies as in a real deduction: `const auto&` makes the object const, a by-value `auto` drops the initializer's const, `auto&&` and `decltype(auto)` keep it, and `auto*` takes the pointee. From a data member, `decltype(auto)` takes the type the member is declared with, or with parentheses the const reference the expression is.",
  "status": "supported",
  "issues": [],
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "main.cpp",
      "text": "template <typename T>\nstruct Vec {\n    T& operator[](int index);\n    const T& operator[](int index) const;\n\n    void push_back(const T& value);\n    int size() const;\n};\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nstruct Grid {\n    Vec<T> cells;\n    Vec<T> spare[2];\n};\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nvoid bar(Vec<Vec<T>> rows,\n         const Vec<Vec<T>>& fixed,\n         Vec<Vec<T>*> pointers,\n         const Grid<T>& grid) {\n    auto& row = rows[0];\n    row.;\n    const auto& view = rows[0];\n    view.;\n    auto copy = fixed[0];\n    copy.;\n    auto&& forwarded = fixed[0];\n    forwarded.;\n    decltype(auto) exact = fixed[0];\n    exact.;\n    auto* pointer = pointers[0];\n    pointer->;\n    decltype(auto) declared = grid.cells;\n    declared.;\n    decltype(auto) named = (grid.cells);\n    named.;\n    auto decayed = grid.spare;\n    decayed->;\n}\n",
